TODO
====

- sig-event-loop-integration: `lib/sig` has no self-pipe/`signalfd` support,
  so a handler installed via `sig_catch`/`sig_action` has to do its work (or
  hand off some other way) from signal-handler context; there's no built-in
  way to turn a caught signal into something a `select`/`poll`/`epoll` loop
  can wait on. Noted while making the module more generally reusable
  (see `doc/sig.md`) -- this is usually the actual reason a server-oriented
  program reaches for a signal library.

- exec-intercept-system-signal-semantics: `exec-intercept.c`'s `system()`/
  `popen()` overrides (used when the map redirects `sh`/`/bin/sh` to another
  shell) reimplement the fork+exec+wait sequence directly instead of calling
  the libc functions, so they skip the POSIX-mandated signal handling around
  the child (`SIGINT`/`SIGQUIT` ignored and `SIGCHLD` blocked in the parent
  for the duration of the call). Fine for the compatibility-testing use case
  this tool targets, but a caller relying on that signal behavior around
  `system()`/`popen()` would see a difference when the shell is redirected.
